{"res":"### Minimal vite-plugin-pwa manifest configuration

Source: https://github.com/vite-pwa/vite-plugin-pwa/blob/main/docs/guide/pwa-minimal-requirements.md

Configures the vite-plugin-pwa plugin with a minimal Web App Manifest. The manifest includes name, short_name, description, theme_color (must match the entry point theme-color), and icons of 192x192 and 512x512. The scope is omitted; the plugin uses the Vite base option (default '/').

```ts
import { VitePWA } from 'vite-plugin-pwa'

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [
    VitePWA({
      includeAssets: ['favicon.ico', 'apple-touch-icon.png', 'masked-icon.svg'],
      manifest: {
        name: 'My Awesome App',
        short_name: 'MyApp',
        description: 'My Awesome App description',
        theme_color: '#ffffff',
        icons: [
          {
            src: 'pwa-192x192.png',
            sizes: '192x192',
            type: 'image/png'
          },
          {
            src: 'pwa-512x512.png',
            sizes: '512x512',
            type: 'image/png'
          }
        ]
      }
    })
  ]
})
```

--------------------------------

### Static assets handling

Source: https://github.com/vite-pwa/vite-plugin-pwa/blob/main/docs/guide/static-assets.md

You can also add another static assets such as `favicon`, `svg` and `font` files using `includeAssets` option. The `includeAssets` option will be resolved using `fast-glob` found under Vite's `publicDir` option directory, and so you can use regular expressions to include those assets, for example: `includeAssets: ['fonts/*.ttf', 'images/*.png']`. You don't need to configure `PWA Manifest icons` on `includeAssets` option.

--------------------------------

### Manifest icons and apple-touch-icon via includeAssets

Source: https://github.com/vite-pwa/vite-plugin-pwa/blob/main/docs/guide/pwa-minimal-requirements.md

VitePWA configuration showing the manifest (icons for the Web App Manifest) alongside `includeAssets` which adds the `apple-touch-icon.png` f"}

